<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lavr Kornilov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lavr Kornilov was a Russian general who became commander-in-chief of the army in July 1917 and was accused of attempting a coup against the Provisional Government in the Kornilov affair that September. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> he helped found the anti-Bolshevik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> in the Don region and led its Ice March into the Kuban. He was killed by a shell during the assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Yekaterinodar</a> in April 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lavr-kornilov.jpg" alt="Lavr Kornilov" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kornilov1916.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lavr Georgiyevich Kornilov was born on 30 August 1870 (18 August in the old calendar) in Ust-Kamenogorsk. Accounts of his ancestry differ: one story makes him a Don Cossack Kalmyk adopted by his mother's brother, while his sister wrote that he had not been adopted and that their mother was of Polish and Altai Oirot descent. He entered military school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omsk">Omsk</a> in 1885 and the Mikhailovsky Artillery School in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a> in 1889, and in 1892 was assigned to the Turkestan Military District, where he led exploration missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-turkestan">Eastern Turkestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and learned several Central Asian languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After graduating from the General Staff Academy in 1897 Kornilov returned to Turkestan as a military intelligence officer. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> he fought at Sandepu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a> and received the Order of St. George, and from 1907 to 1911 he was military attaché in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded the 48th Infantry Division, was captured by the Austrians in April 1915 when his division was cut off, and escaped in July 1916.</p><p>After the abdication of Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> he commanded the Petrograd Military District from March 1917, and on 8 March placed the Empress Alexandra and her children under house arrest. He then took the Eighth Army, whose success in the Kerensky Offensive took 10,000 prisoners before a retreat, and on 24 July took the southern front. A week later he replaced Aleksei Brusilov as supreme commander. Believing the Petrograd Soviet responsible for the army's breakdown, he ordered an assault on it after ambiguous exchanges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kerensky">Alexander Kerensky</a>. The Petrograd Soviet gathered workers and soldiers against him, the coup failed and he was arrested.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Kornilov escaped from the Bykhov jail on 19 November 1917, helped by guards loyal to him, and reached the Don region, where with General Mikhail Alekseev he became military commander of the anti-Bolshevik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a>. He is reported to have promised that the greater the terror, the greater the victories, and bands of his officers killed more than 500 people in the village of Lezhanka.</p><p>On 24 February 1918 he led the army on the Ice March towards the Kuban and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Yekaterinodar</a> on 10 April. A Soviet shell hit his headquarters and killed him on 13 April 1918. Bolshevik troops later dug up his coffin and burned his remains. His legacy is contested: some see a patriot defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s unity, others a reactionary who deepened the country's chaos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lavr Kornilov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It repelled <b>Kornilov</b>'s Volunteer Army at Yekaterinodar in April 1918, where Kornilov was killed.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-soviet-republic">Kuban Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>A failed offensive in June, the July Days and General <b>Lavr Kornilov's</b> attempted march on Petrograd in August weakened it, while the Bolsheviks gained support in the soviets.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-republic">Russian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oskemen, in what is now <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krasnodar">Krasnodar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.95,82.62&amp;year=1901" aria-label="Oskemen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6444 426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lavr Kornilov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6894 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6894 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.95,82.62&amp;year=1901">What was here in 1901? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
